WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.870
Pricing models, value packages, and protecting your scope.

2
00:00:04.220 --> 00:00:08.400
This is the video for Module 2 of Pricing and Value Strategy.

3
00:00:09.650 --> 00:00:16.970
In this module you turn your numbers into offers clients can say yes to. First, choose the right shape for your price.

4
00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:21.120
Then turn a time estimate into a safe fixed quote.

5
00:00:22.370 --> 00:00:25.890
Build three packages based on the value a client expects.

6
00:00:27.140 --> 00:00:30.000
And protect your price when a job starts to grow.

7
00:00:31.250 --> 00:00:43.580
Ask two questions: how clear is the scope, and is the work one-off or ongoing? If you can list exactly what you will deliver, a fixed price works. Clients like knowing the cost in advance.

8
00:00:44.830 --> 00:00:57.220
For ongoing work, like social media or bookkeeping, a monthly retainer gives steadier income. But write down what the month includes, or it slowly becomes unlimited work for a fixed fee.

9
00:00:58.470 --> 00:01:09.610
When tasks are unclear, charge by the hour with a cap, such as up to 20 hours this month. And remember, with fixed prices, getting faster raises what you earn per hour.

10
00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:20.840
Break the job into tasks: briefing, concepts, final design, two revision rounds and handover. Chiamaka estimates 15 hours in total.

11
00:01:22.090 --> 00:01:28.240
15 hours at her minimum rate of 3,800 naira is 57,000 naira.

12
00:01:29.490 --> 00:01:39.540
Then she adds contingency, extra time for slow feedback and things that always take longer. 20% adds 11,400 naira.

13
00:01:40.790 --> 00:01:49.260
That makes 68,400 naira. Her market map shows similar work at about 50,000 to 100,000 naira.

14
00:01:50.510 --> 00:02:01.490
She quotes a clean 70,000 naira, with two revision rounds. Her effective rate is about 4,667 naira an hour, comfortably above her floor.

15
00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:13.710
You cannot guess value. You find it by asking about the client's business. For example, a caterer in Kaduna tells Zainab an average order is about 40,000 naira.

16
00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:19.420
She keeps about 30% as profit, so 12,000 naira per order.

17
00:02:20.670 --> 00:02:26.960
She hopes for 10 extra orders a month. That could be 120,000 naira of extra profit.

18
00:02:28.210 --> 00:02:32.310
Zainab's floor for the work is 57,400 naira.

19
00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:41.260
She prices it at 70,000 naira. That is above her floor, and leaves the client a clear gain if the target is reached.

20
00:02:42.510 --> 00:02:51.640
Sales also depend on the client's product and service. So talk about the value you aim to create, and never write 'guaranteed 10 orders' in a quote.

21
00:02:52.890 --> 00:03:00.400
Three packages let the client choose a level, instead of bargaining over one price. Basic serves price-sensitive buyers.

22
00:03:01.650 --> 00:03:07.190
Standard suits most clients. Seeing a premium option makes the middle one look reasonable.

23
00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:18.340
Premium includes ad management, which the others do not. That difference keeps Standard buyers from expecting ads for free. Every package is above its floor.

24
00:03:19.590 --> 00:03:30.190
Small extra requests can cut your effective hourly rate in half. So list deliverables, revision rounds, turnaround and exclusions in writing before you start.

25
00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:47.420
For example, Zainab's client asks for 3 reels, which are not in the Standard package. She replies kindly: 'It is outside this month's package, so it would be 24,600 naira, or we can swap it for 6 of this month's posts.'

26
00:03:47.770 --> 00:03:51.980
The client pays, and Zainab starts once payment shows in her banking app.

27
00:03:53.230 --> 00:04:02.190
So remember. Decide your change prices before anyone asks. A 50% rush fee is fair, because rushing moves other clients' work.

28
00:04:02.540 --> 00:04:09.770
For projects, ask for a deposit, for example 50%, before starting, and say how long your quote is valid.

29
00:04:11.020 --> 00:04:16.710
Now quote one job two ways, design your three packages, and write your change price list.

30
00:04:17.060 --> 00:04:19.010
Continue with the lessons below.
